why 1 0 DOUBLING Soprano and tenor share C♯ for a beat. The soprano's C♯ falls on to B as part of the tune's descent; only the tenor's C♯ rises to D, as a leading tone should.
why 3 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (C♯–B) and tenor (A–B) both move by step onto B. Bach takes the nearest notes.
why 5 0 DOUBLING Soprano (A–B) and tenor (C♯–B) meet on B from opposite sides by step.
why 5 1/8 OVERLAP Alto and tenor both leap up a sixth, D–B and B–G, to place the G chord higher. They move together, so the order stays clear.
why 5 3/4 SIX_FOUR The ⁶₄ comes on the weak fourth beat and its V on the next downbeat: the cadence is stretched across the bar line, so the tonic lands at the fermata in the middle of bar 6.
why 6 3/4 RANGE The new phrase starts with the bass high on D, stepping down D–C♯–B. Starting high gives the bass room to descend through the phrase.
why 6 7/8 RANGE Still the high start; the bass steps down to B on the next beat.
why 11 0 SPACING The tune peaks on its high G while the alto stays on E. The soprano soon falls F♯–E and the gap closes.
why 11 1/8 SPACING The same gap, closing as the tune falls F♯–E.
why 11 1/4 LEAPS The bass resolves D♯ up to E and then leaps a fourth to A. The tritone D♯–A spans two chords.
why 13 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano and tenor share C♯. The soprano's C♯ passes down to B in the tune, and the tenor's rises to D.
why 14 3/4 SPACING The new phrase opens with soprano and alto close together high up, a bright sound, while the tenor stays low with the bass. The gap closes as the alto descends.
why 14 7/8 SPACING The same bright, high pair over a low tenor.
why 15 1/4 SPACING The tenor drops to F♯ for the B minor chord while the alto stays high on B. At the fermata the tenor is back up on F♯ an octave higher.
why 16 1/4 RANGE The bass climbs B–C♯–D to reach the tonic, then falls back to F♯ for the cadence.
why 16 1/2 RANGE The top of that climb; the bass falls a sixth to F♯ on the next beat.
why 18 1/2 MELODIC The bass drops a seventh, B to C♯, instead of stepping up to C♯, to stay low for the final cadence. The same note an octave lower; it goes on by step to D.
